<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daugavpils</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dvinsk, Dünaburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daugavpils is the second largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> river in the south-eastern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a>. Founded by the Livonian Order as Dünaburg in the 13th century, it later belonged to Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, as Dvinsk. Latvian and Polish forces took it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in January 1920, and it has a large Russian-speaking population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daugavpils.jpg" alt="Daugavpils" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ivo Kruusamägi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daugavpils_luter%C4%81%C5%86u_bazn%C4%ABca_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town's history began in 1273, when the Livonian Order under Ernst von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratzeburg">Ratzeburg</a> built Dünaburg Castle on the site of a Lithuanian settlement, Naujinis, about 18 km from the present city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> ruled the area between 1281 and 1313. In 1577 Ivan the Terrible captured and destroyed the castle, and in the same year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-bathory">Stephen Báthory</a> built a new castle and town 20 km downriver. It received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> town rights in 1582. In 1621 Dyneburg became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflanty-voivodeship">Inflanty Voivodeship</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Russian troops held it, as Borisoglebov, from 1656 to 1667, until the Treaty of Andrusovo returned it to the Commonwealth. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><p>Under Russian rule the fortress was built between 1810 and 1878, and the city was connected to the Saint Petersburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> railway in 1860. It was called Dvinsk from 1893 to 1920. A large Jewish community lived there from the 1780s onwards, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1897 counted 32,400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> among 69,700 inhabitants. Two prominent rabbis lived in the city: Joseph Rosen, the Rogatchover Gaon, and Meir Simcha of Dvinsk. The painter Mark Rothko was born there in 1903. The newly independent Latvian state renamed the city Daugavpils in 1920, after fighting there from 1919 to 1920 among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Soviet troops.</p><p>Soviet rule lasted from 1940 to 1941 and again from 1944 to 1991, with German occupation between 1941 and 1944. After the German invasion of 26 June 1941 the Germans and Latvian collaborators carried out mass executions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and the survivors were forced into a ghetto. Only about 100 survivors remained of a community of 16,000 when the town was liberated in 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Daugavpils is a state city in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> on the Daugava River, the second largest in the country after Riga, which lies some 230 kilometres to the northwest. Its parts north of the river belong to the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> and those to the south to Selonia. It is a major railway junction and industrial centre. The population has long been multicultural: the 1935 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, Russians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a>, and since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the city has had an overwhelmingly Russian-speaking population. Church Hill holds Lutheran, Catholic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Old Believer churches side by side, and the fortress houses the Mark Rothko Art Centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daugavpils” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Soviet government held on in Latgale until Latvian and Polish forces took <b>Daugavpils</b> in January 1920, and it was dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/state/latvian-socialist-soviet-republic">Latvian Socialist Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
